  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on right-skewness—preferring lotteries where more probability is on worse outcomes. That is, it favors more right-skewed distributions, which in this case is Lottery B. The individual does consider that Lottery B has a small chance of a good outcome (0 Euros) and a high chance of a worse outcome (-4000), which aligns with recognizing the right-skewed nature of B. However, the individual's justification appears to be driven by the appeal of the small chance of avoiding loss, not by a preference for right-skewness per se. In fact, the reasoning seems more aligned with a 'hope for the best' heuristic or probability weighting (giving disproportionate weight to rare positive outcomes), rather than a deliberate preference for more right-skewed distributions. The decision reason would prefer B because it is more right-skewed, but the individual seems to prefer B (if at all) because of the small chance of a good outcome, not because it is more right-skewed. The reasoning direction is opposite in spirit: the decision reason values right-skewness as the target, while the individual tolerates it due to the upside. Thus, while the same lottery (B) might be chosen, the underlying rationale differs. The individual does not appear to use skewness as a decision rule.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
